Dense depth supervision for gsplat's simple_trainer.py
(NammaSpace classroom ghosting, docs/ghosting-research-2026-11-24.md, section 2, step 3)

Base: nerfstudio-project/gsplat main at 512d366 (version 1.6.0, 19 Sep 2026).

    cd gsplat && git apply /path/to/gsplat-dense-depth.patch

On an older checkout, try `git apply -3`, or apply by hand. The change is two
Config fields, one Dataset argument, a depth loader and one loss block.

New options
    --depth_dir DIR            directory of <image name without extension>.npy
    --dense_depth_lambda 0.01  weight of the dense depth loss

Depth files (scripts/training/lidar-depth-for-gsplat.py render writes these)
- Camera z-depth (not distance along the ray) in COLMAP model units, float16 or float32.
- Rendered with the original COLMAP camera, distortion included, at any resolution
  with the image's aspect ratio. The loader resizes, undistorts and crops each map
  exactly like its image, always with nearest neighbour.
- 0 where there is no measurement: no mesh hit, or LiDAR never saw the space in front
  of the surface as empty.
- Same relative path as the image: image C1/frame_0001.jpg -> DIR/C1/frame_0001.npy.
- The loader applies the world-normalisation scale itself, so --normalize_world_space
  on or off both work.

Loss: L1 on disparity over measured pixels, times scene_scale: the same form as
gsplat's sparse --depth_loss. It is weighted by exp(-|image gradient|), as in
DN-Splatter's EdgeAwareLogL1, so low-resolution sensor depth counts less at object
edges. A frame with no measured pixel adds zero. TensorBoard: train/dense_depthloss.

Example (the mcmc preset already sets opacity_reg and scale_reg to 0.01):
    python examples/simple_trainer.py mcmc --data_dir DATA --data_factor N \
        --result_dir RESULTS --depth_dir DATA/depths --dense_depth_lambda 0.01
Start at 0.01, then try 0.05 and 0.2. Pick by PSNR/LPIPS on the held-out route clip.

Checked on CPU only, with scripts/training/test-gsplat-dense-depth.py: the depth
warp matches the image warp for distorted and undistorted cameras, and the loss is
zero at a match, ignores unmeasured pixels and never returns NaN. Not yet run on a
GPU or a real scene. Difix3D's trainer is a separate copy of simple_trainer.py and
does not get this change.

